     "Owner requirement 2026-11-13, his words: \"Running that perfect audit round should result in perfect production code\". So the function is anchored on the product, not on the process: the terminal objective is the state of the production code after the round, and every process and machinery term is instrumental to it. The policy file therefore (1) defines perfect production code as the conjunction of every dimension s perfect result, and states what makes that set of dimensions complete: a defect or shortfall of the production code that no dimension would call a failure is a hole in the definition, so the function carries a term for the completeness of the dimension set itself (escapes and owner reports that belong to no dimension, counted per round, target none) and the round-start evaluation must propose the dimension or widening that closes each one; (2) shows the chain from a perfect round to perfect code and names each link that must hold: every item in scope examined, every real defect found, every finding fixed correctly, no fix injecting a defect, nothing merged between the examination and the round s close that was not itself examined; for each link, the term that measures it and the residual probability that it fails; (3) says what one perfect round can and cannot guarantee (a single round reaches perfect code only if the links hold with certainty; where a link is probabilistic the claim is convergence over rounds) and never states the stronger claim where only the weaker is supported.",
     "Owner requirement 2026-11-13, his words: \"In the definition of perfect production code should also be that the integration is perfecty generic and can run every install in a perfect way, resulting in perfect savings\". So the definition of perfect production code has three product terms beyond freedom from defects, each with its perfect end state, its measure and its irreducible floor: (1) generality: the integration runs correctly on every install in the population it claims to support, with no behaviour that is right only for the owner s own hardware, tariff, climate or configuration; state how the install population is enumerated (pump makes and control interfaces, with and without each optional sensor and actuator, tariff and price shapes, climates, house and tank sizes, duty modes, languages, Home Assistant versions), what examined means for a configuration no seat can run on real hardware (simulation, recorded bundles from D15, contributor installs, property tests over the configuration space), and the distance measure (share of the enumerated configuration space exercised end to end; defects found on an install that the owner s install could not have shown); D12 owns generalization today: say what its brief must change to carry this term; (2) operation: on each install the plan the integration executes is the right one and is executed as planned (every actuator write happens, holds and releases as intended; comfort and safety constraints never violated); tie this to the new actuator-write dimension and to D8; (3) savings: the realised cost against the best achievable for that install under its constraints, so perfect savings is zero regret against an oracle plan given the same prices, weather, house and comfort bounds, and against perfect foresight as the stated upper bound; state the enumerator (D0 owns price optimality: its measure, the flat-price null control every quantified savings claim already owes, the measured-against-modelled gap from live diagnostics) and the floor (forecast error, sensor error, pump limits) that no code can remove. For each of the three, say what is NO-READ today and which record or harness would make it readable, and add the acceptance criteria the owner must set (the supported install population; the comfort bounds savings may never trade against) to the Decisions for the owner section.",
     "Owner instruction 2026-11-13, his words: \"Make sure the future rounds can use the audit playbook even with the addition of the objective function and the improved audit dimension. It should also make the round orchestrator pick up the carries and pre-finder carries from the previous round. Also, related to scope during a round, outside and owner filed findings and requests should always fold into the ongoing round if not explicitly deferred by the owner or outsider\". For this group: the round playbook must stay usable after your change lands, so the same pull request adds your step to the playbook (the document a round is run from; the roster group R10-ROUND-PLAYBOOK-FINAL finishes it) as a pointer to the policy file or contract you add, at the place in the round s order where it runs, and your body shows a dry read: starting from the playbook alone, a new orchestrator reaches your step and the instrument that enforces it. If the playbook group has not merged when you hand off, say so and carry the pointer to it.",
